<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Anglo-Afghan War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Anglo-Afghan War was fought between the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. In 1839 a British army, marching through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, invaded, deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a> and installed Shah Shuja. A revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> destroyed the retreating British garrison in January 1842. After a punitive expedition the British withdrew, and Dost Mohammad returned to the throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-anglo-afghan-war.jpg" alt="First Anglo-Afghan War" width="320" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_army_of_the_Indus_entering_Kandahar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The treaty Shuja Shah signed with the British East India Company on 7 June 1809, the first Afghan pact with a European power, pledged joint action against Franco-Persian aggression and foreshadowed the British restoration of Shuja in 1839 and the <b>First Anglo-Afghan War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He was deposed by a British invasion in 1839 during the <b>First Anglo-Afghan War</b> but returned in 1842 and later reunited Kandahar and Herat.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-afghanistan">Emirate of Afghanistan</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>First Anglo-Afghan War</b> the British occupied Kandahar from 1839 to 1842, and the sardars took refuge in Persia before returning.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kandahar">Principality of Kandahar</a></li><li><blockquote>The British forced treaties on the mirs, used Sindh during the <b>First Anglo-Afghan War</b> and then provoked war.</blockquote><a href="/state/talpur-dynasty">Talpur dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
